X86 x86-release-tid-main
(* The tag handoff between the departing and the arriving owner of a slot,
   with upstream's Release() ORDER kept on top of the fix's claim RMW.

   This is the shape that decides whether ThisInstanceProtected() still tells
   the truth after the slot-claim CAS moved off Entry.threadId. The tag is now
   a plain store trailing the claim, so the arriving owner writes it while the
   departing owner's clear of the same field may still be in flight.

   P0 is the departing owner. It publishes the slot free (lce=0) and then
   clears its tag (tid=0). P1 is the arriving owner: it claims the slot with a
   locked RMW and stamps its own tag.

   No weak memory is needed. On x86 the release store is an ordinary MOV, so
   P0's two stores leave the store buffer in program order and the free is
   visible while the clear is still pending -- long enough for P1 to claim,
   tag, and then have P0's clear land on top of its tag.

   The condition is the resurrection: the slot is owned by P1 (lce=1) yet
   carries no tag (tid=0). Every later ThisInstanceProtected() by P1 answers
   "not protected" while P1 holds the slot, because nothing rewrites tid until
   the slot changes hands again. *)
{ lce=0; tid=0; }
 P0            | P1                ;
 MOV [lce],$0  | MOV EDX,$1        ;
 MOV [tid],$0  | XCHG [lce],EDX    ;
               | MOV [tid],$2      ;
exists (lce=1 /\ tid=0)
